WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.338 --> 00:00:02.921
(bright music)

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.690
- [Narrator] Plastics have become

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.570
such an entrenched part of our lives

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.710
but what exactly is plastic
and how was it made?

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.930
Before plastic became so
ubiquitous, it underwent

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:24.750
a transformation from being
a strictly natural product

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.470
to being synthetically
and widely produced.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.220
Some of the earliest uses of plastic

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.200
date as far back as 3,500 years ago

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.180
when the Olmecs of Mexico used
naturally occurring plastics,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.220
sap from gum trees, to
create rubber balls.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.860
During the mid-19th and 20th centuries,

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.880
synthetic plastics like
celluloid and Bakelite,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.710
made their debut and
were used for decades.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.290
On the molecular level,
plastics are made of polymers

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.320
which are long flexible
chains of chemical compounds.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.150
This structure allows
plastics to be easily molded

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.660
and shaped, especially,
under heat and pressure.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.980
Unlike the rubber used by the Olmecs,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.660
most of today's plastics are manmade

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.990
and derived from fossil fuels.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.510
Crude oil and natural
gas are primary sources

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.530
as they provide a cheap alternative

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.220
to plastic made from plants.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.090
The first step in the
production of plastic

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.140
is the extraction of
crude oil and natural gas

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.530
from the ground.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.700
From there, the fossil
fuels are sent to refineries

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.380
where they are converted
into several products

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.320
including the building blocks of plastic,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.770
ethane from crude oil and
propane from natural gas.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:48.680
Ethane and propane are then
sent to a cracker plant

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.450
to be cracked or broken
down into smaller molecules.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.000
Ethane produces ethylene and
propane becomes propylene.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.060
Next, a catalyst is mixed in which links

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.410
the molecules together and
forms polymers called resins.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.260
This structure allows
plastics to be easily molded

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:11.630
and shaped, especially,
under heat and pressure.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.590
Polymerization converts ethylene

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.560
into the resin, polyethylene,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.710
and propylene into polypropylene.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.930
These resins are then melted,
cooled down and chopped up

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.370
into preproduction plastic
pellets known as nurdles.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.490
Nurdles are later
transported to manufacturers

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:34.300
who use heat to mold the
nurdles into different types

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.600
of plastic products.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.130
Many of those products
actually feature information

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.310
about their manufacturing process.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.190
Numbers called resin identification
codes are often featured

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:49.420
indicating which chemicals
were used to make the plastic.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.320
Because of plastics utility,
it's global production

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.230
has doubled about every decade.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.440
The amount of plastics produced since 1950

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.720
has measured at about 9.2 billion tons,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.720
the weight of nearly 1,600
Great Pyramids of Giza.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.640
Much of this plastic ends up as trash.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.830
Single-use plastic such
as straws, grocery bags

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.360
and packaging products are,
particularly, detrimental.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.280
They often aren't recycled
and constitute about 40%

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.030
of all plastic waste.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.990
They end up damaging natural
habitats, endangering wildlife

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.230
and polluting communities
around the world.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.000
Moving forward, a great way we can counter

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.590
this plastic pollution
is to reduce the amount

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.390
of single-use plastics we use.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.060
By opting, instead, for
reusable alternatives

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.060
in our day-to-day lives, each
person can make a huge impact

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.380
in helping decrease plastic waste.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:59.620
For plastics that are already produced,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.600
other solutions are being explored.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.280
Scientists have discovered
that a few organisms

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:09.010
are capable of breaking
down plastic material.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.550
Wax worms and mealworms, for
example, can devour plastics

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.060
and turn them into compost.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:20.270
Another plastic-consuming
organism is a microbe

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.930
which shrinks the time
plastic takes to degrade

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.840
from hundreds of years to only a few days.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.930
Change is happening in the
production of plastics as well

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.210
with some manufacturers turning back

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.940
to plants for ingredients.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.670
Called biodegradable bioplastics,

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:42.080
these materials are as
durable as synthetic plastics,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.100
come from renewable resources

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.110
and can, therefore, biodegrade.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.380
Even the rubber tree, the
same plant used by the Olmecs,

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.540
is, once again, being sourced for plastic.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.700
Today, the tree's latex is used to create

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.970
a range of products such as all
natural latex rubber gloves,

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.720
tires and mattresses.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.190
The rubber tree itself is now
part of a global initiative

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:13.050
for the natural and sustainable
sourcing of plastic.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.210
Plastics can be found almost anywhere

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.650
but by understanding its chemical makeup

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:27.500
and our uses for it, we can
benefit from its versatility

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:31.590
while also being mindful of
its impact on our planet.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.850
Take your pledge today to
reduce single-use plastics.

